    "case_id": "751",
    "case_summary": "A 41-year-old Saudi male presented to the emergency department with lethargy, fever (38.5°C), progressively worsening chronic dry cough with shortness of breath, night sweats, and unintentional weight loss of 7 kg over two months. Medical history included homosexuality, smoking, and keeping cats as pets at home.\n\nVital signs: Temperature 38.9°C, heart rate 128/min, respiratory rate 28/min, blood pressure 108/65 mmHg, oxygen saturation 88% on room air. Patient appeared cachectic with muscle wasting and poor oral hygiene including oral thrush. Glasgow Coma Scale 13/15, pupils reactive and equal, no focal neurological deficits. Chest exam showed dullness over right lower lung zone with reduced tactile vocal fremitus, decreased breath sounds, fine basal crepitations and increased vocal resonance.\n\nLaboratory Tests:\n- WBC: 13.3 k/μl (86% neutrophils)\n- Hemoglobin: 6.1 g/dL\n- Platelets: 86,000/μl\n- Reticulocyte count: 0.3%\n- VBG: pH 7.45, PCO2 37mmHg, PO2 25mmHg, HCO3 25meq/L\n- Fibrinogen: 760 mg/dL\n- CRP: 40 mg/dL\n- ESR: 79\n- Procalcitonin: 1.5 ng/ml\n\nImaging Studies:\n1. Chest X-ray:\n- Title: Initial Chest Radiograph\n- Description: Opacification in right hemithorax with pleural effusion, left lung mid-wedge-shaped opacification\n\n2. CT Angiography:\n- Title: Chest CT Angiography\n- Description: No pulmonary embolism, large area of non-enhancement within right lower lobe lung parenchyma suggesting liquefication related to infection versus necrosis\n\n3. Brain MRI:\n- Title: Brain MRI with contrast\n- Description: Three well-defined lesions within right basal ganglia and medial aspect of right superior frontal gyrus (10.8*1.8*1.4 cm, 0.7*0.8*0.5cm, and 1.4*1.4*0.8cm) showing hyperintense T2, hypointense T1 with diffusion restriction and peripheral nodular enhancement, associated with extensive perifocal edema involving right frontotemporal parietal lobe",
